#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092120 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac7f7e-1e07-57d7-a82a-d6545588c2de" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "5d2c0fff-c9af-5e8a-846a-640878f08283" :question/math-academy-id "e-730" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x) = \\ln(x^2-4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = \\ln u, \\qquad u = x^2-4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{u},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092122 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "7df7fed3-c172-5cc2-aca8-abd31a0902a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-731" :question/problem "If ![](images/8e/8ee06a44a28a99f50bb9c7e6e0e631681bd19d03f51d3f89999a5d5465ab28ca.svg) what is ![](images/8b/8bd2b0cdc118445cb7209a061d1e689083ef8665bf91dcb6d0e16ff8f893ff79.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we express ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) as the composite function\n\n![](images/f5/f5dffc31aa9f9e4800bf188e5cbe5594b795f7bd6e152d995dcb892aa750747c.svg)\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n![](images/66/6686a165e62ff0e0a972191edbad7e6ce46f54ba090f9e1ff9ee4e35c619e551.svg)\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n![](images/69/69511a4ca68a7d4ca4f46ca95bdd0527723eac03d7bb5150f43dc0c630be27b2.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac7f7e-1e07-57d7-a82a-d6545588c2de" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c711c6-c26a-500b-b638-ad365d5497b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525219} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa7ff7e-f8bc-561e-9cf1-6528723d5c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-3x}{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525241} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf7d78b-c45e-53f7-999c-4a80ff37e483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4-3x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525440} {:answer/id #uuid "b838064a-d671-59c1-b665-3fcd391b15d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525678} {:answer/id #uuid "e8094c2d-9f63-5a0d-ad04-932d633f5305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{4-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bf7d78b-c45e-53f7-999c-4a80ff37e483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4-3x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525440} :answer-field/id #uuid "820a30d1-df8f-5a80-a8f9-d041f414d3e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c35e6115-b838-577e-80b7-a11c81db3ab4" :question/math-academy-id "q-48000" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ for $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-3x)+3x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-3x)$ and $h(x)=3x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x)+h(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we differentiate $g(x).$ We express $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-3x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=\\operatorname{ln}⁡u,\\,u=4-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{u} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate $h(x)=3x:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=3\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x)+{h}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4-3x}+3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070b1605-3f0f-5052-807c-9e2da26f1b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{(5x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525221} {:answer/id #uuid "2d8415e7-55ff-5ae3-b4f6-b66c8c1d541d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{(5x-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525312} {:answer/id #uuid "4f82f8a5-4d83-5410-bc3d-5d0dbb832acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{(2x-5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525407} {:answer/id #uuid "7e998750-c647-5300-8e4d-ccc8bad0a0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(5x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525520} {:answer/id #uuid "845c0b40-9827-530c-894b-969a75a5872a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(5x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "070b1605-3f0f-5052-807c-9e2da26f1b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{(5x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525221} :answer-field/id #uuid "80eb6a81-e7b7-57ed-8210-04192f46d9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6612d2f1-5666-54fd-9c5c-1217f3a2dfe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-48008" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={log}_{3}⁡(5x-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{3}⁡(u),\\,u=5x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(5x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac7f7e-1e07-57d7-a82a-d6545588c2de" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b051919-3827-5d44-b953-53aa435d5e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{3-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525309} {:answer/id #uuid "35305753-bf59-5a3f-9947-5ec6098ae826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525340} {:answer/id #uuid "5058c0f3-ac14-5d64-9985-830088c4dbcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{3-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525411} {:answer/id #uuid "b5548668-dee0-5c14-ac46-d0e79d900df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525672} {:answer/id #uuid "ddbccd9d-4028-5763-81d7-e7870c0ffed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b051919-3827-5d44-b953-53aa435d5e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{3-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525309} :answer-field/id #uuid "0954b44e-1f83-5f7e-addf-a9663bd41e56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48c79b64-abbc-5e4d-8dc8-b69b110491e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-48009" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ for $f(x)=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2x)+7.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2x)+7$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡u+7,\\,u=3-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{5}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{u}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{u} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{3-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64f2aaec-bdad-58bd-9357-db2f7aec0e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525462} {:answer/id #uuid "660e5a36-c7b3-5888-86e1-6b4e9b38a638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525467} {:answer/id #uuid "8bbc0fd9-f288-5653-866a-e4f8e72943d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(2x+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525554} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6cb00b-824e-53d9-8528-2efc1a6d7e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(2x+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525600} {:answer/id #uuid "abec248d-6ade-55e3-bdab-e993fd571336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{2x+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "660e5a36-c7b3-5888-86e1-6b4e9b38a638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525467} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e53e3f5-af7a-50b0-9c33-a1155d8c0ec1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b5dd752-1f0d-5a16-86ac-253b01c9d23a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71483" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y={log}_{2}⁡(2x+3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{2}⁡(u),\\,u=2x+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
